## 🧠 2. EXPERT ANALYSIS

Your field notes capture a very clear look at how your brain builds an internal map of your body when interacting with an unyielding environment like pavement. When you plant your feet firmly on solid ground and twist, the pavement pushes back with equal force (ground reaction force), making your structural stability very high-contrast. Contracting like a worm forces your deep spinal muscles to work hard in a shortened position against this solid baseline. This intense compression alters the local sensory signals, making that side of your back feel strangely thick, or "padded." 

The mental lag you felt when straightening up with your eyes closed shows that your internal map does not update instantly. When you hold an extreme twist on solid ground, the nerves in your joints and muscles adjust to that position as the "new normal." When you physically return to center without your eyes open to confirm it, your brain takes a few minutes to clear out the old signals and realize you are straight again. Unlocking your knees simply forces your entire back to take over the workload of fighting gravity on the hard pavement, which is why your whole posture suddenly feels heavy and integrated.

